  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 239 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA dry pattern is expected to continue for this weekend through early \nnext week. Temperatures will continue to warm up on Saturday as an \nupper-level system is expected to move out of the Rockies and \napproach northwest Texas.  The surface low associated with this \nsystem will move into the Panhandle by Saturday afternoon, which \nwill increase southwest winds during the day. At this point, models \nshow winds of 15-20 MPH with gusts around 30 in the afternoon. \nModels currently show the aforementioned system pushing a strong \ncold front into Texas late Saturday night through Sunday morning. \nWinds behind the front on Sunday morning also look to be breezy from \nthe north at 15-25 MPH with gusts over 30.  However, winds could end \nup stronger as they will be driven by a mid-level jet streak of 50-\n70 mph winds. Otherwise, precipitation chances are low (<10%) with \nthis front but high temperatures should be held down to the mid 50s \nduring the day on Sunday due to cold advection. Temperatures should \nrecover rather quickly into the 60s and 70s for early to middle of \nnext week as southerly flow resumes.  Model ensembles show the next \npotential chance for rain at 10-20% for Wednesday night with the \napproach of the next system.",
